course: dictionary
lesson: 9434
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Stagger is a good example. «Before they set off, the Rat comes back staggering under a fat wicker picnic basket.» Here stagger means walking unsteadily, almost falling, because the basket is so heavy.
explain: Stagger can also mean to shock or amaze someone. «The Mole is staggered at the size, the extent, the ramifications of it all» means he is amazed, not that he is wobbling. Ask yourself: is the sentence describing a body moving, or a feeling? That question sorts the meanings.
explain: To check your choice, put the meaning into the sentence. "The Rat comes back amazing under a fat picnic basket" does not make sense, so the walking meaning wins. Stagger can also be a noun, meaning an unsteady walk.
fact: Stagger is a verb with several meanings, and it can also be a noun: a stagger.
fact: To stagger means to walk or move unsteadily, almost falling, because you are hurt, tired, dizzy or carrying something heavy.
fact: «Staggered» can mean shocked or amazed: the Mole is «staggered at the size» of the passages.
fact: Staggered can also mean arranged so that things do not all happen at once, like «staggered lunch breaks».
fact: In a race, a staggered start puts the outer lane runners ahead, because the outer track is longer, so the race is fair.
exercise: «Before they set off, the Rat comes back staggering under a fat wicker picnic basket.» What does staggering mean here? | Hiding quickly behind a tree | *Walking unsteadily under a heavy load | Singing happily as he walks
exercise: «The Mole is staggered at the size of it all.» What does staggered mean here? | Dizzy from spinning around | *Shocked and amazed by how big it is | Arranged in a neat line
exercise: «Our school has staggered lunch breaks so the canteen is not crowded.» What does staggered mean here? | Walked unsteadily | Cancelled for the day | *Arranged so they do not all happen at the same time | Shocked and amazed
test: Ella says, «After the race she came back with a stagger.» What is a stagger? | *An unsteady walk, almost falling | A loud shout across the field | A sharp pain in the knee | A long race around the track
test: At the start of a race, the outer lane runners go first because their track is longer. Why is the start staggered? | So the crowd can walk out early | *So every runner has a fair race | So the runners get tired faster | So everyone runs in a circle
exam: Which meaning fits «The sheer size of the cake staggered the children»? | Made the children laugh loudly | Arranged the children in neat lines | *Amazed and shocked the children | Made the children walk unsteadily
